From e2d343693601aa42971c8d9be0c068d2ddc5ccd9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:08:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayerSubtitle: a text line read before a seek comes
 back if it is on screen at the target

After a seek the FFmpeg demuxer reads again from the video keyframe at or
before the target. In Matroska it starts at the cluster of that keyframe, so
a subtitle line stored before it (it started earlier) is not read again,
even if it is still on screen at the target. The text codecs (SubRip, plain
text, WebVTT from FFmpeg's demuxer, MP4 timed text) drop every line in the
flush of the seek, so that line is missing until the next one starts. Seen
with FFmpeg 9.0.2 on a Matroska file with a keyframe every 10 s: after seeks
to 11, 21, 30.5 and 52 s the line on screen had started at 8, 18.5, 29 and
45 s, and none of them was read again. MP4 seeks every stream on its own and
does read it.

CSubtitleSeekMemory keeps, for the open text stream, its packets as they
were read: 2 MiB at most, within 10 minutes of film time around the newest,
only packets with a start and a duration and without a timestamp correction
of the player (a correction empties it). After a seek, FlushBuffers() asks
the subtitle player for those that start at or before the target (less the
subtitle delay) and end later than 20 s before it, and they are decoded
again. The packets the demuxer then reads again are recognised by time,
duration and data and not decoded twice. It holds only what was read from
the file, so it gives back what a read from the start of the line gives.

ASS keeps its libass track on a flush (CDVDOverlayCodecSSA::Flush() does not
flush the events) and is left as it was. Image subtitles are not touched.
Sources that are realtime are left as they were.

Not covered: a seek forward into a part not read yet. Reading the subtitles
before the target there means reading the file from the cluster of the
subtitle (FFmpeg reads every block of a cluster, discarded or not): tens of
MB per seek in the test file.

kodi.log, info level, once per seek that gives something back:
"CVideoPlayerSubtitle: after the seek to T s, N subtitle packet(s) read
before it given back (...)".
